From a4a1444e5fe8f202c0942cd73a666bc573d714e9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tillmann Severin <tillmann.severin@mailbox.org>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 17:07:33 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 39/49] media: dvb-frontends: add a driver for the AltoBeam
 ATBM7821

The ATBM7821 is the DVB-T2/T/C demodulator in Hi3798MV200 receivers
such as the Octagon SF8008, with a MaxLinear MxL608 tuner in front of
it.  Neither part is publicly documented and no source for either
exists outside a vendor binary, so the register sequences here were
recorded from the I2C bus while the vendor's driver tuned a channel.
Those captures are published with the tooling that made them.

Three things about this hardware shape the driver.

The tuner sits behind a gate inside the demodulator and the
demodulator takes its clock from the tuner's crystal, so the two
cannot usefully be separate drivers: stopping the tuner's digital core
stops the demodulator, which closes the gate, which removes the only
route back to the tuner.  Only removing power recovers from that, so
there is no power-down path here at all and sleep does nothing.

The demodulator is never told the symbol rate or the constellation.
It searches for both.  It is not told the frequency either; only the
tuner is.  A tune is therefore a tuner operation followed by the
restart of a search that configures itself, and a channel reaches the
hardware through four tuner registers - a frequency in sixty-fourths
of a megahertz and two band thresholds.

Reads of the DVB-C core go through a window which has to be latched
first, or the four bytes are sampled as they change and do not belong
together.  The related ATBM888X latches elsewhere, which is worth
stating because following that part's documentation returns bytes that
are not the register at all.

The adapter is registered by the frontend driver, which is not where
it belongs.  This receiver has no driver for its demultiplexer yet, so
there is nothing to attach to, and this at least makes the frontend
reachable.  It should move once that driver exists.

DVB-C only for now; the chip also does T and T2.

Co-authored-by: Copilot <223556219+Copilot@users.noreply.github.com>
